{{also|Ming|mīng|míng|mǐng|mìng|Míng}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|/mɪŋ/}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|mingen|lang=enm}}, {{term|mengen|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|mengan||to mix, combine, unite, associate with, consort, cohabit with, disturb, converse|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Germanic|mangijanan|to mix, knead|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|menk-|to rumple, knead|lang=en}}. Cognate with Dutch {{term|mengen||to mix, blend, mingle|lang=nl}}, German {{term|mengen||to mix|lang=de}}, Danish {{term|mænge||to rub|lang=da}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|gemang|ġemang|mixture, union, troop, crowd, multitude, congregation, assembly, business, cohabitation|lang=ang}}. More at {{l|en|among}}.

====Alternative forms====
* [[minge]], [[meng]]

====Verb====
{{en-verb|mings|minging|'''[[minged]]''', '''[[meint]]''', or '''[[ment]]'''}}

# {{context|now|_|rare}} To [[mix]], [[blend]], [[mingle]].
# {{obsolete}} To bring (people, animals etc.) [[together]]; to be [[joined]], in [[marriage]] or [[sexual intercourse]].
#* '''1590''', Edmund Spenser, ''The Faerie Queene'', I.ii:
#*: the old man [...] him brought into a secret part, / Where that false couple were full closely '''ment''' / In wanton lust and lewd embracement [...].
# {{UK|dialectal}} To [[produce]] through [[mixing]]; especially, to [[knead]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# [[mixture|Mixture]].

===Etymology 2===
Backformation from {{term|minging}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{British|slang}} To be unattractive (person or object).
# {{British|slang}} To be foul smelling.

=====Related terms=====
* [[minger]]
* [[minging]]

===Etymology 3===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|mingen|lang=enm}}, {{term|mengen|lang=enm}}, {{term|mungen|lang=enm}}, {{term|muneȝen|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|myngian|lang=ang}}, {{term|mynegian|lang=ang}}, {{term|gemynegian|ġemynegian|to bring to mind, have in mind|lang=ang}}, from {{term|myne||mind|lang=ang}}, from {{term|gemunan|ġemunan|to remember|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Germanic|munanan|to think|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|men-|to think|lang=en}}. Merged in Middle English with {{etyl|ang}} {{term|gemyndgian|ġemyndgian|to remember, be mindful, remind, intend, commemorate, mention, exhort, impel, warn, demand payment|lang=ang}}. More at {{l|en|mind}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|minge}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{transitive}} To [[speak of]]; [[mention]]; [[tell]]; [[relate]].  
# {{intransitive}} To [[speak]]; [[tell]]; [[talk]]; [[discourse]].

